the eastern Mediterranean energy  Leadership Summit in Athens on Tuesday  was dominated by Cyprus two years from  now the Mediterranean island is aiming  to join neighbours Israel and Egypt in  being energy independent and an exporter  of natural gas that's not going down  well in Turkey it wants an agreement  with the islands Turkish Cypriot  community on how the energy wealth will  be shared  Turkish ships are exploring for oil and  gas within the area Cyprus has  demarcated as its exclusive economic  zone some experts predict the political  insecurity will hamper development using  gunboat diplomacy in raising the threat  of escalation and war  it makes it impossible for any rational  actor like companies to make the  long-term investment Turkish troops  invaded Cyprus in 1974 in response to a  Greek coup attempt on the island Turkey  controls more than a third of the  divided island where the Turkish Cypriot  minority lives a political settlement  remains elusive with the Republic of  Cyprus dominated by Greek Cypriots the  Cypriot government is offering to open a  bank account to deposit 30 percent of  oil and gas revenues for Turkish  Cypriots until a political agreement is  reached but turkeys government says that  is a unilateral change to the status quo  we kept warning the Greek Cypriots  do not take any unilateral steps which  would a infringe on Turkish continental  shelf rights be on the rights of the  Turkish Cypriots on the North because it  is not enough to say look don't worry  I'm going to give you a share of any  proceeds Turkey also disagrees with the  area Cyprus is demarcated with its  neighbours saying it should be a lot  smaller if we accept the Turkish version  of the Cypriot exclusive economic zone  we will be left with 31% what it  actually is today  all of the discoveries in Cyprus so far  will all fall in other countries and and  I think turkey must first answer the  question how is that in the interest of  Turkish Cypriots much is at stake  according to the United States  Geological Survey the eastern  Mediterranean holds 350 trillion cubic  feet of natural gas and three and a half  billion barrels of oil that alone is  enough to power the entire European  Union for 20 years at the moment Cyprus  Egypt and Israel stand to share most of  those resources and turkey has none of  them the overarching question is whether  Turkey will demarcate its maritime  interests according to the international  law of the sea like its neighbours or  raise the temperature further john  psaropoulos al jazeera athens
 

